gs."]}, "overview": "Hawaii is among the most reliably Democratic states in presidential politics, and the partisan lean of roughly 13 points is the anchor for this open-seat forecast. With the incumbent term-limited, neither side gains an incumbency edge, and no candidate-specific fundraising or outside spending figures are available to shift the picture.\n\nNo Hawaii polls, expert ratings, or prediction-market pricing have been published for this race, so the forecast rests entirely on fundamentals. That thin evidence base means the expected margin stays at the state's lean, but uncertainty remains wide, and a strong national environment for one party could move the actual result meaningfully in either direction.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9253, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8734, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8703, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9295, "margin": 16.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.874, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.8784, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.877, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.874, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.8862, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.878, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.8719, "margin": 13.0}]}